1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:06,500
In the heart of a hospital, a room where every second counts.

2
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:12,840
Doctors, medicines, and machines in a battle to preserve life.

3
00:00:16,840 --> 00:00:21,100
Now, Emergency Room on Modern Marvels.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:42,340
A critically injured patient arrives at University of California Davis Medical Center in Sacramento,

5
00:00:42,860 --> 00:00:46,020
one of the busiest emergency rooms in the country.

6
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:54,880
Air ambulance paramedics have stabilized the patient during the helicopter flight with oxygen and fluids.

7
00:00:56,260 --> 00:00:59,260
For the medical team, time is of the essence.

8
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,140
This could be a case of life or death.

9
00:01:03,141 --> 00:01:05,960
I'm a doctor. I'm trained to save lives.

10
00:01:06,480 --> 00:01:07,700
To me, death is a defeat.

11
00:01:08,600 --> 00:01:12,160
How can I turn my back on something, no matter how hopeless it seems?

12
00:01:19,600 --> 00:01:23,800
Practicing emergency medicine is like being on the front lines of war.

13
00:01:24,480 --> 00:01:26,680
We've been around for thousands of years,

14
00:01:26,681 --> 00:01:30,740
and we've been at the battlefront for thousands of years as well.

15
00:01:35,640 --> 00:01:43,880
Hippocrates, when he was talking about how you can really and truly develop the expertise as a physician,

16
00:01:44,080 --> 00:01:45,600
well, he said you need to go to the battlefield.

17
00:01:45,840 --> 00:01:51,800
Well, what he was talking about was people providing acute and emergency care at the battlefront.

18
00:01:52,320 --> 00:01:54,420
And that was, that's an ER doc.

19
00:01:54,421 --> 00:01:59,640
100 million Americans will visit an emergency room this year.

20
00:02:00,400 --> 00:02:02,560
That is one out of every three people.

21
00:02:03,460 --> 00:02:05,220
Some will go for minor problems.

22
00:02:06,920 --> 00:02:09,820
Others will have a life-threatening injury or illness.

23
00:02:11,460 --> 00:02:12,940
138 over 118.

24
00:02:13,160 --> 00:02:13,860
Okay.

25
00:02:14,080 --> 00:02:19,000
In an extreme trauma situation, the team uses the latest in medical technology.

26
00:02:19,001 --> 00:02:23,500
from monitoring devices that provide information on blood pressure,

27
00:02:23,980 --> 00:02:26,540
oxygen levels, and heart rhythms of the patient,

28
00:02:26,900 --> 00:02:34,060
to intravenous medications like reperfusing enzymes D5W and heparin.

29
00:02:34,800 --> 00:02:35,500
Don't move.

30
00:02:35,501 --> 00:02:36,201
Ouch.

31
00:02:37,660 --> 00:02:38,400
There you go.

32
00:02:39,140 --> 00:02:43,780
Once the patient is stable, more detailed diagnostic work can begin.

33
00:02:44,520 --> 00:02:45,860
It's controlled chaos.

34
00:02:46,260 --> 00:02:48,140
Believe it or not, we really do know what we're doing.

35
00:02:48,141 --> 00:02:51,800
Even amidst all the helter-skelter that's going on, there is a plan.

36
00:02:51,940 --> 00:02:55,380
And we know exactly what's going on with everybody at any given time.

37
00:02:55,680 --> 00:02:58,920
We better know what we're doing with all this technology.

38
00:03:04,260 --> 00:03:10,500
This patient has been stabilized and is now on his way to the operating room for the next phase of treatment.

39
00:03:12,660 --> 00:03:17,780
In emergency medicine, the most severe injuries are labeled level one traumas.

40
00:03:18,140 --> 00:03:23,920
A level one trauma facility must have a full surgical team available 24 hours a day.

41
00:03:24,920 --> 00:03:30,800
And there are specific criteria for the types of cases that are taken to a level one hospital.

42
00:03:31,800 --> 00:03:33,640
The whole idea is instability.

43
00:03:33,641 --> 00:03:38,940
In other words, patients who are injured seriously enough to have significant blood loss,

44
00:03:39,280 --> 00:03:44,260
to be in a life-threatening situation, any penetrating wounds,

45
00:03:44,360 --> 00:03:47,720
either stab wounds or gunshot wounds of the chest or abdomen,

46
00:03:47,721 --> 00:03:52,560
multiple long bone fractures, patients with severe head injuries,

47
00:03:53,120 --> 00:03:55,880
head injuries with loss of consciousness.

48
00:03:56,700 --> 00:04:02,060
The treatment of patients with severe trauma injuries has evolved over thousands of years.

49
00:04:03,520 --> 00:04:06,220
Archaeological sites dating back to the Stone Age

50
00:04:06,221 --> 00:04:09,360
reveal bodies that showed some type of medical attention.

51
00:04:09,880 --> 00:04:14,580
I will argue that clearly emergency medicine is the oldest specialty.

52
00:04:14,581 --> 00:04:18,580
when the first caveman dropped a rock on his foot

53
00:04:18,581 --> 00:04:21,940
or when flesh met flesh, when flesh met tooth,

54
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:27,400
when flesh met a rock and there was a laceration, an injury,

55
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:30,360
and one of that caveman's compatriots

56
00:04:30,361 --> 00:04:32,260
tried to do something about that injury,

57
00:04:32,540 --> 00:04:34,920
that was the first emergency medicine practitioner.

58
00:04:35,980 --> 00:04:38,800
Prehistoric man treated wounds with plasters

59
00:04:38,801 --> 00:04:41,060
of plant and animal extracts

60
00:04:41,061 --> 00:04:43,200
and then covered them with bandages.

61
00:04:43,201 --> 00:04:50,200
There is some research that theorizes that early man set broken limbs and reset dislocated joints.

62
00:04:50,201 --> 00:04:57,200
Well, I would think that amongst the first injuries you see are orthopedic injuries.

63
00:04:57,201 --> 00:05:02,200
Fractures are pretty obvious even without x-rays.

64
00:05:02,201 --> 00:05:10,200
Obviously the subtle fractures aren't, but the knowledge of when and how to splint people while they heal

65
00:05:10,201 --> 00:05:13,000
probably is very rudimentary knowledge.

66
00:05:13,760 --> 00:05:16,460
By 1200 BC, in ancient Greece,

67
00:05:16,960 --> 00:05:19,880
wounded men were taken to the temple of Asclepius,

68
00:05:20,320 --> 00:05:23,100
the son of the god Apollo and a mortal woman.

69
00:05:23,780 --> 00:05:26,180
The patients would go to a sleeping room in the temple

70
00:05:26,181 --> 00:05:27,940
where it was believed they would be healed

71
00:05:27,941 --> 00:05:30,100
through a divinely inspired dream.

72
00:05:30,860 --> 00:05:33,360
The legendary medical teacher Hippocrates

73
00:05:33,361 --> 00:05:36,720
traced his origins back to descendants of Asclepius.

74
00:05:37,620 --> 00:05:41,280
Hippocrates embraced the art of observation and medical diagnosis

75
00:05:41,281 --> 00:05:45,040
and warned his students to first, do no harm.

76
00:05:46,520 --> 00:05:51,020
Divine intervention for healing purposes was frequently invoked in ancient Greece.

77
00:05:51,620 --> 00:05:55,420
But some concrete medical knowledge did develop on the battlefield.

78
00:05:56,500 --> 00:06:01,780
Injured soldiers were given a drink of opium mixed with wine to kill their pain.

79
00:06:01,781 --> 00:06:06,860
and the wounds themselves were covered with sap or honey to stop the bleeding.

80
00:06:10,280 --> 00:06:15,020
But it was the Roman Empire that first employed full-time medical personnel

81
00:06:15,021 --> 00:06:19,680
to travel with their legions and administer to injured soldiers during battle.

82
00:06:21,440 --> 00:06:27,200
In the Middle Ages, medical practitioners developed the use of the sling bandage for arm injuries

83
00:06:27,201 --> 00:06:30,780
and weighed and pulled traction devices for leg fractures.

84
00:06:30,781 --> 00:06:38,120
But the invention of gunpowder brought with it a new set of problems in the care of severe penetrating wounds.

85
00:06:38,800 --> 00:06:42,980
Some of the management of wounds in the Middle Ages is pretty horrifying

86
00:06:42,981 --> 00:06:46,340
because they thought that you had to cauterize every wound.

87
00:06:46,960 --> 00:06:50,460
And then they would wait for the wound to get infected.

88
00:06:51,740 --> 00:06:55,620
Anboise Paré, a French surgeon in the 1500s,

89
00:06:56,040 --> 00:07:00,340
revolutionized the treatment of wounds by using tourniquets to stop the bleeding.

90
00:07:00,780 --> 00:07:06,120
He simply cleaned and wrapped the injured area with gauze and let the body heal itself naturally.

91
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:11,400
This procedure preempted a lot of amputations due to unnecessary infections.

92
00:07:13,960 --> 00:07:19,580
New techniques for treating a variety of emergency cases continued in the 16th century.

93
00:07:20,720 --> 00:07:26,660
Physicians at that time developed a crude bellows to pump air into a person who had stopped breathing.

94
00:07:26,661 --> 00:07:33,040
Mouth-to-mouth resuscitation and CPR would not be used until the 1950s.

95
00:07:33,900 --> 00:07:41,500
During the French Revolution, Dominique Jean-Lauret realized that time was a crucial factor in treating wounded soldiers.

96
00:07:42,400 --> 00:07:50,340
He devised the concept of the ambulance volante, a version of the horse and buggy that would transport surgeons to the front lines.

97
00:07:51,060 --> 00:07:54,860
When civil war was declared in America in the mid-1800s,

98
00:07:54,861 --> 00:07:59,600
neither side was prepared with a strategy for evacuating wounded soldiers.

99
00:08:00,220 --> 00:08:02,720
Some were left helpless on the battlefield for weeks.

100
00:08:03,660 --> 00:08:10,580
Well, the civil war was as horrifying an experience in disaster medicine

101
00:08:10,581 --> 00:08:13,340
that this country has ever seen.

102
00:08:14,140 --> 00:08:16,980
And when you consider the sheer numbers of casualties

103
00:08:16,981 --> 00:08:20,720
in the major battles of the Civil War.

104
00:08:20,721 --> 00:08:25,100
It simply was overwhelming to the medical care at the time.

105
00:08:26,400 --> 00:08:30,600
Jonathan Letterman was brought in to head up the medical corps for the union,

106
00:08:31,080 --> 00:08:34,720
and he organized teams of ambulances for transporting the injured

107
00:08:34,721 --> 00:08:38,620
and a system of dressing stations in nearby field hospitals.

108
00:08:39,580 --> 00:08:44,560
The field surgeons performed their amputations under extremely unsanitary conditions.

109
00:08:45,200 --> 00:08:49,020
Instruments and sponges were reused without cleaning them between patients.

110
00:08:49,760 --> 00:08:52,780
And the surgeries were performed on patients who were conscious.

111
00:08:54,180 --> 00:08:58,940
At that time, we didn't have much to do for an injury other than amputation.

112
00:08:59,500 --> 00:09:03,200
And we didn't have a lot of anesthesia with which to amputate.

113
00:09:03,300 --> 00:09:11,180
So there were a lot of people who probably died from the painful experience of having a leg amputated.

114
00:09:11,181 --> 00:09:18,640
It did improve the surgical technique of many of the battlefield surgeons.

115
00:09:18,820 --> 00:09:21,440
They could amputate a leg in less than a minute.

116
00:09:22,020 --> 00:09:25,540
But that wasn't necessarily the best way to do it.

117
00:09:26,380 --> 00:09:31,480
And I think that what we learned from that war the hard way

118
00:09:31,481 --> 00:09:35,660
was that we need a better way of getting casualties to medical care

119
00:09:35,661 --> 00:09:37,900
and better medical care.

120
00:09:37,901 --> 00:09:47,900
The hundreds of thousands of casualties during the Civil War caused the medical profession to begin to look for new ways to provide emergency services.

121
00:09:47,901 --> 00:09:52,900
But in the late 1800s, there were only three permanent hospitals in the United States.

122
00:09:52,901 --> 00:10:01,900
And it wasn't until World War II that there were any significant technological advances in acute care procedures.

123
00:10:01,901 --> 00:10:10,620
procedures. Hammurabi's code of written laws compiled around 1600 BC included a

124
00:10:10,621 --> 00:10:16,080
list of prices physicians could charge for different services. Emergency Room

125
00:10:16,081 --> 00:10:24,100
will continue on Modern Marvels. We now return to Emergency Room on Modern

126
00:10:24,101 --> 00:10:31,060
Marvels. The practice of on-scene emergency medicine was developing during

127
00:10:31,061 --> 00:10:33,060
during World War II.

128
00:10:33,061 --> 00:10:36,060
It was the first time doctors and medics on the front lines

129
00:10:36,061 --> 00:10:40,060
gave blood and other fluid therapies to wounded soldiers.

130
00:10:42,060 --> 00:10:47,060
But until the 1950s, amputation was still the only answer

131
00:10:47,061 --> 00:10:50,060
for most of the trauma encountered on the battlefield.

132
00:10:50,061 --> 00:10:56,060
It wasn't really until Korea and the latter half of this century

133
00:10:56,061 --> 00:11:00,060
that we developed techniques of being able to work with arterial injuries

134
00:11:00,061 --> 00:11:04,800
injuries and limb salvage, that was one of the most important advances during the Korean conflict.

135
00:11:05,960 --> 00:11:19,960
Well, knowledge comes from a variety of resources, and it is grimly ironic that some of our major advances in the management of death and disease is from military

136
00:11:21,345 --> 00:11:22,200
medicine.

137
00:11:22,201 --> 00:11:28,440
But while the level of military medicine progressed for the first half of the 20th century,

138
00:11:29,120 --> 00:11:32,420
emergency care on the home front had fallen behind.

139
00:11:33,060 --> 00:11:36,780
During that period of time, it was pretty dismal.

140
00:11:37,380 --> 00:11:43,560
And the reason is that we did not have an enormous amount of civilian trauma.

141
00:11:44,160 --> 00:11:47,740
We didn't have a really great highway system.

142
00:11:47,741 --> 00:11:53,320
We didn't have an enormous number of automobile accidents, although we had them.

143
00:11:54,040 --> 00:11:59,080
Many of the major accidents that occurred just simply killed people

144
00:11:59,081 --> 00:12:03,160
because there was no way to get them from where they were being injured

145
00:12:03,161 --> 00:12:05,120
to a place that could take care of them.

146
00:12:05,960 --> 00:12:09,740
Many of the medical problems, the non-traumatic problems,

147
00:12:10,160 --> 00:12:16,420
were cared for only late in their course inside of a hospital.

148
00:12:16,421 --> 00:12:20,360
and they were not cared for in an emergency department

149
00:12:20,361 --> 00:12:23,740
because doctors still made house calls.

150
00:12:24,800 --> 00:12:28,180
Through the 1960s, emergency medicine in America

151
00:12:28,181 --> 00:12:31,700
was poor in its quality and small in its scope.

152
00:12:32,360 --> 00:12:37,180
But several factors converged to bring about a significant change in its practice.

153
00:12:37,880 --> 00:12:40,920
The thing that resulted in emergency medicine development

154
00:12:40,921 --> 00:12:43,500
was the passage of Medicaid and Medicare

155
00:12:43,501 --> 00:12:48,500
because in years prior to the advent of health insurance,

156
00:12:48,501 --> 00:12:52,500
most of the people who used emergency rooms were indigent.

157
00:12:52,501 --> 00:13:00,500
Or conversely, since they had no previous contact with the physicians involved in the emergency practice,

158
00:13:00,501 --> 00:13:03,500
were often hard to collect money from.

159
00:13:03,501 --> 00:13:06,500
So private hospitals did not have emergency rooms.

160
00:13:06,501 --> 00:13:09,500
According to studies conducted by Dr. Blaisdell,

161
00:13:09,501 --> 00:13:10,201
Dr.

162
00:13:10,202 --> 00:13:15,340
Blaisdell, the need for emergency medicine changed drastically as a result of the hippie

163
00:13:15,341 --> 00:13:18,461
movement and the protests of the Vietnam War.

164
00:13:18,462 --> 00:13:21,720
These protests escalated and brought with them,

165
00:13:21,840 --> 00:13:23,448
paradoxically, violence.

166
00:13:23,449 --> 00:13:28,340
Now the violence occurred because in parallel with the development of the

167
00:13:28,341 --> 00:13:32,851
Hyde-Ashbury concept, hard drugs were introduced into the city of San Francisco.

168
00:13:32,852 --> 00:13:33,640
The drug trade

169
00:13:33,641 --> 00:13:37,174
want violence into our American cities that we never had before.

170
00:13:37,175 --> 00:13:38,720
We know, on the basis of our

171
00:13:38,721 --> 00:13:42,840
statistics at San Francisco General Hospital where we had all the injuries and so forth,

172
00:13:42,920 --> 00:13:45,218
that crimes of violence were rare.

173
00:13:45,219 --> 00:13:49,340
One or two a week in the city of San Francisco prior to 1968.

174
00:13:50,040 --> 00:13:54,464
All of a sudden, overnight, crimes of violence doubled. And they doubled again.

175
00:13:54,465 --> 00:13:55,360
And they doubled

176
00:13:55,361 --> 00:13:58,553
again. And shootings and stabbings were rampant.

177
00:13:58,554 --> 00:14:01,880
There were bombs set off in the police department.

178
00:14:02,660 --> 00:14:05,461
Downtown Detroit was essentially burned down.

179
00:14:05,462 --> 00:14:08,200
And that precipitated a whole reorganization

180
00:14:08,201 --> 00:14:11,060
of services because instead of being an occasional thing,

181
00:14:11,580 --> 00:14:13,760
now injuries were a regular thing.

182
00:14:14,800 --> 00:14:17,460
The need for more effective emergency services

183
00:14:17,461 --> 00:14:19,640
inspired a group of concerned doctors

184
00:14:19,641 --> 00:14:23,500
to start the American College of Emergency Physicians

185
00:14:23,501 --> 00:14:27,220
to train doctors for their work in the emergency department.

186
00:14:28,280 --> 00:14:29,880
Prior to this initiative,

187
00:14:30,400 --> 00:14:33,300
the doctors staffing these departments were ill-equipped

188
00:14:33,301 --> 00:14:35,300
to deal with the majority of the problems

189
00:14:35,301 --> 00:14:37,140
that presented themselves at their door.

190
00:14:38,200 --> 00:14:43,600
One night it would be a surgeon, one night it would be a urologist, one night it would be a radiologist.

191
00:14:44,100 --> 00:14:51,200
Well, in the private sector, many of these specialists said, look, I don't know anything about traumatic care.

192
00:14:51,280 --> 00:14:53,609
I don't know anything about medical problems.

193
00:14:53,610 --> 00:14:55,680
I'm a psychiatrist or I'm a pathologist.

194
00:14:56,300 --> 00:15:00,140
It doesn't make sense for me to be here as well as it's bad for patient care.

195
00:15:01,160 --> 00:15:05,420
Emergency medicine became a bona fide specialty in 1979.

196
00:15:05,421 --> 00:15:11,880
These newly trained specialists are career professionals in emergency room medicine and

197
00:15:11,881 --> 00:15:16,960
with the help of new technology are able to perform more specific treatments than in the

198
00:15:16,961 --> 00:15:17,960
past.

199
00:15:17,961 --> 00:15:22,540
We've got all the technology that's available in the hospital at our fingertips here in the

200
00:15:22,541 --> 00:15:25,140
emergency department for cardiac monitoring.

201
00:15:25,141 --> 00:15:30,040
We put those little sticky things on your chest and we then connect it to a special machine

202
00:15:30,041 --> 00:15:34,220
that will monitor everything from the heartbeats that you have

203
00:15:34,221 --> 00:15:37,740
to your blood pressure to the amount of oxygen in your blood.

204
00:15:37,741 --> 00:15:40,180
So we try to use all that stuff to our best advantage

205
00:15:40,181 --> 00:15:41,980
to take good care of you.

206
00:15:41,981 --> 00:15:45,360
The electrocardiograph records electrical changes

207
00:15:45,361 --> 00:15:47,580
in the heart and is used to monitor

208
00:15:47,581 --> 00:15:50,700
and diagnose any abnormalities.

209
00:15:50,701 --> 00:15:53,400
The first electrocardiograph required the patient

210
00:15:53,401 --> 00:15:56,740
to place his hands and feet in buckets of water

211
00:15:56,741 --> 00:16:01,340
to conduct the current to a galvanometer for measurement.

212
00:16:01,341 --> 00:16:05,140
It was invented by the Dutch physiologist Willem Einhoven

213
00:16:05,141 --> 00:16:07,860
in 1901.

214
00:16:07,861 --> 00:16:11,040
Another vital piece of the emergency medical care puzzle

215
00:16:11,041 --> 00:16:13,060
is blood pressure.

216
00:16:13,061 --> 00:16:17,380
In 1896, Italian physician Riva Ricci

217
00:16:17,381 --> 00:16:21,300
developed the sphygmomanometer to measure the force of blood

218
00:16:21,301 --> 00:16:23,440
flowing from the heart.

219
00:16:23,441 --> 00:16:26,560
Today, through the use of a pulse oximeter,

220
00:16:26,561 --> 00:16:32,880
Doctors can find out much more information about the circulation of the blood and its oxygen levels.

221
00:16:34,380 --> 00:16:40,760
The device measures light absorption through the blood vessels in the finger to gather its information.

222
00:16:46,140 --> 00:16:52,660
Wilhelm Rehnken's discovery of X-ray technology in the late 1890s helped physicians see for themselves

223
00:16:52,661 --> 00:16:57,320
any fractures or foreign objects that may have lodged internally in the body.

224
00:17:03,500 --> 00:17:09,680
Since the 1970s, emergency doctors also use computerized axial tomography

225
00:17:09,681 --> 00:17:13,280
to get a faster and more comprehensive look at patients

226
00:17:13,281 --> 00:17:16,140
that helps in making a more rapid diagnosis.

227
00:17:17,640 --> 00:17:22,220
CT scan is a special type of x-ray that cuts patients like a loaf of bread.

228
00:17:22,221 --> 00:17:27,540
and you can identify specific injuries with CT scanning.

229
00:17:28,060 --> 00:17:32,160
At first, and greatest value initially, was in evaluating head injury.

230
00:17:32,860 --> 00:17:37,280
The head was a black box, and you really, standard x-rays,

231
00:17:37,680 --> 00:17:42,120
could not really tell you anything about the nature of the injury inside the head.

232
00:17:43,040 --> 00:17:45,040
With the advent of the CT scan, all of a sudden,

233
00:17:45,380 --> 00:17:47,540
you could see everything was going on inside the head.

234
00:17:47,660 --> 00:17:49,700
See small hemorrhages, major hemorrhages.

235
00:17:50,540 --> 00:17:52,040
I'd say that was a great advance.

236
00:17:52,041 --> 00:17:57,240
Subsequently, apply to the abdomen, where you could diagnose liver injuries and spleen injuries

237
00:17:57,241 --> 00:18:01,340
without having to wait until the patient bled enough to drop their blood count

238
00:18:01,341 --> 00:18:03,520
to tell you that there was serious internal hemorrhage.

239
00:18:07,060 --> 00:18:12,680
The emergency department staff must be ready to handle all of the cases all of the time.

240
00:18:13,240 --> 00:18:15,140
They are trained in the art of triage,

241
00:18:15,141 --> 00:18:20,340
or sorting out the most serious cases from the ones that can be handled in a less urgent manner.

242
00:18:20,341 --> 00:18:25,260
The patients come to us and one of three things happens.

243
00:18:25,560 --> 00:18:31,620
They either are treated and we send them home, they're admitted upstairs, or they die.

244
00:18:32,160 --> 00:18:33,340
Just one of three things.

245
00:18:33,500 --> 00:18:36,880
And our goal is to get them out of the emergency department as quickly as possible.

246
00:18:37,260 --> 00:18:38,260
It's very exciting.

247
00:18:38,420 --> 00:18:40,800
We see what I love to call the soup to nuts of medicine.

248
00:18:40,940 --> 00:18:41,900
It's a little bit of everything.

249
00:18:42,460 --> 00:18:46,440
We see people from the beginning of life to the end of life.

250
00:18:46,441 --> 00:18:51,440
Level one traumas pull the staff of the emergency department together.

251
00:18:51,441 --> 00:18:56,440
The surgical resident heads up the team, along with other doctors, registered nurses,

252
00:18:56,441 --> 00:19:01,440
a radiology technician, and respiratory therapist.

253
00:19:01,441 --> 00:19:04,440
The charge nurse coordinates from the sidelines.

254
00:19:04,441 --> 00:19:09,440
It's what I call the full-court press, and everybody is there to stabilize the patient.

255
00:19:09,441 --> 00:19:15,440
In emergency medicine, I'm constantly interacting with the paramedics who bring the patient.

256
00:19:15,441 --> 00:19:21,500
I'm constantly interacting with the respiratory technicians and the x-ray technicians,

257
00:19:21,720 --> 00:19:26,220
as well as with the consulting services to whom we're admitting patients.

258
00:19:26,700 --> 00:19:30,200
So you have to learn how to get along with people,

259
00:19:30,360 --> 00:19:32,860
and doctors have never been taught that very well.

260
00:19:33,440 --> 00:19:35,260
But in emergency medicine, it's critical.

261
00:19:35,860 --> 00:19:39,160
This patient was attacked and is now unconscious.

262
00:19:40,020 --> 00:19:43,780
His chest wall is stable. His breast signs are equal bilaterally.

263
00:19:43,781 --> 00:19:46,120
He's nasally intubated to the right and airing.

264
00:19:46,121 --> 00:19:51,280
The trauma team works together to try to get a complete assessment of all the patient's

265
00:19:51,281 --> 00:19:56,560
life-threatening injuries and get him the treatment that he needs as quickly as possible.

266
00:19:56,561 --> 00:20:03,900
He has a large basilar skull fracture extending into his left carotid canal.

267
00:20:04,520 --> 00:20:07,900
He has a right cerebellar hemorrhage contusion.

268
00:20:08,640 --> 00:20:11,160
He has a fracture in his brain and magnum.

269
00:20:11,161 --> 00:20:16,820
He has blood in his sphenoid sinus and air in his pituitary closet.

270
00:20:17,320 --> 00:20:23,180
The vast majority of these patients were healthy people minding their own business.

271
00:20:23,940 --> 00:20:28,020
Until in a split second, they've had this devastating injury.

272
00:20:28,720 --> 00:20:32,520
And sometimes we can restore them back to complete function.

273
00:20:32,940 --> 00:20:34,020
Sometimes we can't.

274
00:20:34,021 --> 00:20:42,800
So I think that our goal is try to get them back as whole as they were before this injury.

275
00:20:43,340 --> 00:20:48,980
When appropriate, the doctors and nurses do try to get information directly from the patient.

276
00:20:49,500 --> 00:20:51,300
But sometimes that is impossible.

277
00:20:53,280 --> 00:20:56,760
That's an important attribute of an emergency physician

278
00:20:56,761 --> 00:21:01,800
to immediately establish a human contact with a patient

279
00:21:01,801 --> 00:21:03,860
who, no matter how trivial the problem,

280
00:21:04,200 --> 00:21:07,420
probably has some fear and needs some reassurance.

281
00:21:07,600 --> 00:21:12,140
But there are times when the golden rule does not apply in medicine,

282
00:21:12,560 --> 00:21:14,680
and you just have to do things to people

283
00:21:14,681 --> 00:21:16,920
because that's what's going to keep them alive.

284
00:21:16,921 --> 00:21:20,360
The normal tone is decreased when he's in parallel.

285
00:21:21,180 --> 00:21:24,380
Emergency medicine is a highly stressful specialty,

286
00:21:24,940 --> 00:21:28,740
and the doctors and nurses who practice it often suffer from burnout.

287
00:21:28,741 --> 00:21:36,980
I'll tell you where I think it stems from is the intensity of their work style, if you will.

288
00:21:37,660 --> 00:21:40,520
Always being the one to deal with the cardiac arrest,

289
00:21:40,720 --> 00:21:45,520
or always being the one to deal with the life-threatening choking or massive bleeding.

290
00:21:46,020 --> 00:21:50,080
And that takes its emotional toll when you're dealing with that all the time.

291
00:21:51,180 --> 00:21:57,400
I think particularly when we have a child or a baby, that stays with everybody.

292
00:21:57,401 --> 00:22:01,060
and it's very, very, very hard to get through.

293
00:22:01,460 --> 00:22:05,400
And many times people have to take a shift off to be able to deal with that.

294
00:22:06,080 --> 00:22:09,160
So I think it's the nature of what we do

295
00:22:09,161 --> 00:22:13,280
and the intensity of it at times and the life and death.

296
00:22:14,980 --> 00:22:17,960
Emergency medicine is not an exact science.

297
00:22:18,460 --> 00:22:20,640
It borrows from other areas of medicine

298
00:22:20,641 --> 00:22:23,980
and applies the necessary procedures as called for.

299
00:22:23,981 --> 00:22:29,740
By and large, most of the technology that we use and the medications that we use are

300
00:22:29,741 --> 00:22:35,340
used because they have been developed for other areas of expertise, the cardiologists,

301
00:22:35,341 --> 00:22:39,820
the neurologists, the surgeons, and we get to take the best of all their technologies

302
00:22:39,821 --> 00:22:44,820
and use them in the acute care setting in the emergency department.

303
00:22:44,821 --> 00:22:51,180
Since 1979, the physicians in the emergency department are qualified to perform more technical

304
00:22:51,181 --> 00:22:52,900
procedures than ever before.

305
00:22:53,960 --> 00:22:59,980
If it looks as if you're severely depleted of oxygen, we give our first drug of choice

306
00:22:59,981 --> 00:23:02,600
oxygen, because oxygen is a drug.

307
00:23:03,040 --> 00:23:08,780
If that's not working, then we will put a tube in called an endotracheal tube, and we

308
00:23:08,781 --> 00:23:11,840
will then put you on a ventilator, and that will breathe for you, or we will sit there

309
00:23:11,841 --> 00:23:15,240
with a bag and we'll squeeze it and we'll force oxygen into your lungs.

310
00:23:15,241 --> 00:23:23,540
The intubation procedure consists of inserting a tube through a laryngoscope into a patient's windpipe.

311
00:23:24,780 --> 00:23:28,740
The technique was brought to America from Europe in 1880.

312
00:23:31,960 --> 00:23:34,700
The emergency department is not going to go away.

313
00:23:35,500 --> 00:23:41,720
I think that it requires sophisticated equipment and technology to support it.

314
00:23:41,721 --> 00:23:47,300
It requires people who are trained, people who want to be there, people who know how to be there.

315
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:55,400
And my dream is that someday in this country, you won't go into an emergency department anywhere in the country

316
00:23:55,401 --> 00:24:00,800
and be met by anything other than a resident trained graduate in emergency medicine.

317
00:24:01,500 --> 00:24:02,380
We're not there yet.

318
00:24:06,940 --> 00:24:11,340
St. Vincent's Hospital is a level one trauma center in New York City.

319
00:24:11,720 --> 00:24:16,840
Its reputation as one of the top emergency rooms goes back to its beginnings.

320
00:24:17,660 --> 00:24:21,720
The Sisters of Charity started the hospital in 1849.

321
00:24:23,280 --> 00:24:26,780
Their first emergency ward opened in 1890.

322
00:24:31,760 --> 00:24:36,740
The hospital's original location was in the West Village section of New York.

323
00:24:37,680 --> 00:24:42,420
In 1849, the area was more like a bustling seaport village

324
00:24:42,421 --> 00:24:44,420
than the big city that we know today.

325
00:24:45,180 --> 00:24:49,360
And the hospital's patients were mostly newly arrived immigrants.

326
00:24:51,340 --> 00:24:55,680
The Sisters of Charity were initially assisted by eminent physicians

327
00:24:55,681 --> 00:25:00,200
in the New York area, most notably Dr. Valentine Montt,

328
00:25:00,201 --> 00:25:02,720
called the father of American surgery.

329
00:25:02,721 --> 00:25:03,627
And while St.

330
00:25:03,628 --> 00:25:09,420
Vincent's had the assistance of some of the greatest minds in medicine at the time,

331
00:25:10,100 --> 00:25:14,080
the critical care techniques can only be described as crude at best.

332
00:25:14,680 --> 00:25:25,700
They didn't have developed anesthesia, so they used alcohol sometimes to anesthetize or numb the patient before surgery.

333
00:25:26,080 --> 00:25:30,860
Sometimes they had to do amputations if there was a very bad wound to a limb.

334
00:25:30,861 --> 00:25:38,840
they used leeches. In 1875 the Sisters of St. Vincent's pioneered the

335
00:25:38,841 --> 00:25:43,100
establishment of the New York Board of Health to help in the fight against

336
00:25:43,101 --> 00:25:47,260
infectious diseases that spread throughout the city. I think there were a

337
00:25:47,261 --> 00:25:54,140
lot of standards that kind of grew out of the everyday practice that emanated

338
00:25:54,141 --> 00:25:59,846
from the hospital. One of my favorite stories is during the 1850s.

339
00:25:59,847 --> 00:26:02,180
This was before the days of

340
00:26:02,181 --> 00:26:06,140
Lister, who discovered the microbes in the lab.

341
00:26:06,141 --> 00:26:10,100
The doctors came in in their street clothes and

342
00:26:10,101 --> 00:26:15,400
operated on patients, and the sisters insisted that the doctors at their hospital wash their

343
00:26:15,401 --> 00:26:17,233
hands when they came in.

344
00:26:17,234 --> 00:26:22,580
The infection rate and the deaths due to post-operative infections was

345
00:26:22,581 --> 00:26:24,380
fall lower at the Sisters Hospital.

346
00:26:25,320 --> 00:26:28,640
St. Vincent's Hospital was the second hospital in New York

347
00:26:28,641 --> 00:26:31,760
to use a horse-drawn ambulance in 1870,

348
00:26:32,700 --> 00:26:36,780
starting one of the oldest traditions of emergency medical services in the country.

349
00:26:37,460 --> 00:26:41,260
I believe Bellevue Hospital had the first horse-drawn ambulance by history,

350
00:26:41,400 --> 00:26:42,400
but we were soon after.

351
00:26:43,320 --> 00:26:47,420
It wasn't like a Kentucky Derby, but we followed quickly with our horse-drawn ambulance.

352
00:26:48,200 --> 00:26:49,800
And they progressed over the years.

353
00:26:49,801 --> 00:26:54,800
St. Vincent's did have the first motorized ambulance in 1900.

354
00:26:54,801 --> 00:26:58,800
It was the Model T Ford, I think.

355
00:26:58,801 --> 00:27:05,800
It's amusing that that was a big step forward when we look back to say there was a motorized ambulance.

356
00:27:05,801 --> 00:27:09,800
In fact, the people during those days put it aside after a few months

357
00:27:09,801 --> 00:27:15,800
because they found it less reliable than the horse that they could just go out and jump on.

358
00:27:15,801 --> 00:27:22,280
jump on and the horse would always run but the mechanical motorized wouldn't always turn over

359
00:27:23,400 --> 00:27:28,680
saint vincent's was called to help save the victims of the triangle shirtwaist fire of 1911

360
00:27:29,400 --> 00:27:35,560
and in 1912 the hospital provided beds to over 100 survivors of the titanic disaster

361
00:27:37,240 --> 00:27:43,320
their medical teams provided on-scene emergency care and ambulance transport in 1945

362
00:27:43,321 --> 00:27:46,880
when a military plane crashed into the Empire State Building.

363
00:27:47,880 --> 00:27:51,900
In the late 1940s, St. Vincent's developed what would become

364
00:27:51,901 --> 00:27:54,800
one of the premier trauma care facilities in New York,

365
00:27:55,500 --> 00:27:57,380
thanks to Sister Loretta Bernard,

366
00:27:57,381 --> 00:28:01,380
who ran the hospital from 1939 until 1960.

367
00:28:03,040 --> 00:28:06,600
She recognized St. Vincent's potential.

368
00:28:07,460 --> 00:28:10,980
She recruited physicians of worldwide fame.

369
00:28:10,981 --> 00:28:21,580
So we had a neurosurgeon, an eye surgeon, a head and neck surgeon, and a general surgeon.

370
00:28:22,600 --> 00:28:26,780
Sister Margareta Brock has been at St. Vincent's for over 60 years.

371
00:28:27,500 --> 00:28:31,780
She acts as an advocate for patients and their families in the emergency department.

372
00:28:32,380 --> 00:28:38,340
When the World Trade Building was bombed, I shall never forget the number of patients we got.

373
00:28:38,341 --> 00:28:44,060
children were lost because the parents were taken in the ambulance too quickly

374
00:28:44,061 --> 00:28:49,060
or they were in the elevator waiting to be picked up by their parents.

375
00:28:49,640 --> 00:28:54,840
And that was a very traumatic experience for the patients.

376
00:28:54,841 --> 00:29:02,100
But thank God we could really and truly substitute for a parent on many occasions.

377
00:29:02,940 --> 00:29:06,600
Because of her long-term experience administering care,

378
00:29:07,220 --> 00:29:11,145
Sister Margareta has an expanded view of the people who come to St.

379
00:29:11,146 --> 00:29:12,200
Vincent's for help

380
00:29:12,201 --> 00:29:14,880
and the purpose of the emergency department.

381
00:29:16,080 --> 00:29:19,120
There's many stories you could tell about the homeless.

382
00:29:19,121 --> 00:29:22,160
The big thing is many times they just need support.

383
00:29:22,700 --> 00:29:25,600
They need clothing. They need something to eat.

384
00:29:26,280 --> 00:29:31,160
And that's part of emergency care regardless of what anybody says.

385
00:29:31,161 --> 00:29:38,300
The emergency department at St. Vincent's is named after Dr. William O'Loughlin,

386
00:29:38,301 --> 00:29:41,240
the ship physician who died on the Titanic.

387
00:29:41,960 --> 00:29:46,140
Emergency Room will continue in a moment on Modern Marvels.

388
00:29:46,640 --> 00:29:51,620
We now return to Emergency Room on Modern Marvels.

389
00:29:52,400 --> 00:29:57,940
An integral part of emergency medicine is the transporting of patients to the hospital.

390
00:29:58,680 --> 00:30:03,100
The faster the victim gets to the hospital, the more chance he or she has for survival.

391
00:30:03,980 --> 00:30:08,440
The timely delivery of injured parties goes back to the Napoleonic Wars.

392
00:30:09,320 --> 00:30:14,800
Napoleon came up with the idea of transporting his wounded off the battlefield in hot air balloons

393
00:30:14,801 --> 00:30:17,820
to a place where they could receive medical attention.

394
00:30:18,620 --> 00:30:26,240
He started a balloon factory in 1794 for that purpose, but never actually used one in battle.

395
00:30:27,940 --> 00:30:39,660
In the Vietnam War, casualties were assessed on the spot, and the survivors were carried to the nearest evacuation hospital for rapid treatment.

396
00:30:40,520 --> 00:30:45,300
Doctors and medics staffed the helicopters and began administering care during the transport.

397
00:30:47,180 --> 00:30:54,680
The concept of stabilizing the patient en route to the hospital was introduced in the United States in 1969.

398
00:30:54,681 --> 00:31:03,460
30, 40 years ago we had gurney jockeys or ambulance drivers who would just arrive on scene,

399
00:31:03,600 --> 00:31:08,260
throw a patient on a stretcher in the back of a Cadillac and off the way they go to the hospital

400
00:31:08,261 --> 00:31:10,800
without any real care being administered to that patient.

401
00:31:11,760 --> 00:31:15,960
In the 1960s, ambulances were provided through mortuary companies.

402
00:31:16,720 --> 00:31:20,580
These vehicles were actually hearses with sirens and a paint job.

403
00:31:20,780 --> 00:31:23,540
So you know what? Gee, how wonderful it was.

404
00:31:23,541 --> 00:31:25,500
there wasn't a whole lot of emergency care provided,

405
00:31:25,600 --> 00:31:26,560
and if you had a bad outcome,

406
00:31:26,640 --> 00:31:28,660
they could just kind of take you right in that same vehicle

407
00:31:28,661 --> 00:31:31,240
and bring you to the local mortuaries, all the same people.

408
00:31:32,440 --> 00:31:37,420
In 1968, Dr. J. Frank Pantridge of Belfast, Ireland,

409
00:31:37,620 --> 00:31:40,560
realized that people were dying of heart attacks

410
00:31:40,561 --> 00:31:43,940
because they were not transported to the hospital quickly enough

411
00:31:43,941 --> 00:31:46,980
and because they were not getting care en route.

412
00:31:50,120 --> 00:31:51,760
So this Dr. Pantridge said, you know what?

413
00:31:51,761 --> 00:31:56,680
We're going to put doctors on ambulances, and when we get someone that sounds like they're having a heart attack, we're going to go there.

414
00:31:58,100 --> 00:32:04,260
Pantridge and his partner, John Getty, set up a vehicle equipped with a battery-operated portable defibrillator,

415
00:32:05,080 --> 00:32:13,780
endotracheal tubes, and suction apparatus, and a supply of drugs that would normally be available in a coronary care hospital unit.

416
00:32:15,980 --> 00:32:19,020
They used a shortwave radio for communications.

417
00:32:19,021 --> 00:32:24,020
Their work reduced the amount of fatalities from heart attacks.

418
00:32:24,021 --> 00:32:28,020
Of the first 13 patients they visited, 10 survived.

419
00:32:28,021 --> 00:32:29,632
Dr. William Grace of St.

420
00:32:29,633 --> 00:32:37,020
Vincent's Hospital went over to Ireland and came back with a similar plan for the ambulances in New York City.

421
00:32:37,021 --> 00:32:43,020
It was the first mobile coronary care unit ambulance in the United States.

422
00:32:43,021 --> 00:32:47,480
and really, I'm going to say selfishly for Dr. Grace and St. Vincent's,

423
00:32:47,481 --> 00:32:51,740
became the model for paramedic-manned ambulances in this country.

424
00:32:56,640 --> 00:33:01,940
In the last 30 years, paramedic training has grown to include certain procedures

425
00:33:01,941 --> 00:33:04,820
that previously could only be done in a hospital setting.

426
00:33:05,980 --> 00:33:10,940
Once on the scene, the first priority is to physically stabilize the patient.

427
00:33:10,941 --> 00:33:15,720
This boy was hit by a car while riding his bike.

428
00:33:16,840 --> 00:33:21,580
The paramedics use a cervical collar and backboard to support his neck and back.

429
00:33:26,900 --> 00:33:33,420
At times, the paramedics will start an intravenous line to provide medication on the way to the hospital.

430
00:33:34,240 --> 00:33:36,440
It's going to feel like a small little bee sting, okay?

431
00:33:40,940 --> 00:33:48,720
The paramedics also carry a life pack, which includes a defibrillator for extreme heart

432
00:33:48,721 --> 00:33:57,500
attack emergencies, and a portable electrocardiograph to monitor the patient's heart rate.

433
00:33:57,501 --> 00:34:03,060
The readout is transmitted through telemetry, and the paramedics use the telephone to inform

434
00:34:03,061 --> 00:34:06,180
the hospital of the condition of the incoming patient.

435
00:34:06,181 --> 00:34:09,300
This patient's chief complaint today is out of chest pain.

436
00:34:10,300 --> 00:34:18,080
In a sharp substernal pain underneath his left breast, radiating down his left arm and into his left jaw.

437
00:34:19,280 --> 00:34:26,400
Advances in communication systems have helped to bridge the crucial time factor in extreme emergency situations.

438
00:34:27,480 --> 00:34:36,140
Less than 200 years ago in the 19th century, town criers walked the streets to announce any emergencies that occurred.

439
00:34:37,040 --> 00:34:43,560
The invention of the telephone in 1875 made it possible to call a doctor directly

440
00:34:43,561 --> 00:34:45,660
who would then come to your house.

441
00:34:53,460 --> 00:34:57,400
Telephones became common in households in the 1950s,

442
00:34:57,401 --> 00:35:01,640
and people would call the police if there was an accident or other type of trauma.

443
00:35:01,641 --> 00:35:12,640
The first 911 emergency system was introduced in Haleyville, Alabama on February 16th, 1968.

444
00:35:12,641 --> 00:35:16,640
911, this is Jan, what are you reporting?

445
00:35:16,641 --> 00:35:26,640
Today, the 911 system is a countrywide organized network that dispatches the ambulances and fire departments that are closest to the scene.

446
00:35:31,640 --> 00:35:45,640
The 911 operators who answer the calls are trained in emergency services and procedures.

447
00:35:50,640 --> 00:35:55,640
Emergency medical dispatcher training has increased significantly in the past 10 years

448
00:35:55,641 --> 00:36:00,640
and I feel it's provided and given us in the field a greater support and backbone

449
00:36:00,641 --> 00:36:02,580
for us to provide our job in the field.

450
00:36:03,700 --> 00:36:07,740
Better communications, quicker response times,

451
00:36:07,960 --> 00:36:12,900
and medical care on the scene are all factors that come together

452
00:36:12,901 --> 00:36:15,620
to create more efficient pre-hospital care.

453
00:36:16,380 --> 00:36:18,500
And there is the human element as well.

454
00:36:19,700 --> 00:36:21,320
Do you know where you are right now?

455
00:36:22,540 --> 00:36:23,260
Where?

456
00:36:24,660 --> 00:36:25,360
Where?

457
00:36:29,000 --> 00:36:29,700
No?

458
00:36:29,701 --> 00:36:32,660
Okay, I want you to just sit back and relax, okay?

459
00:36:33,220 --> 00:36:35,960
We're going to take you on over to the hospital, all right?

460
00:36:36,480 --> 00:36:41,120
It's very difficult not to become personally involved with some of the patients

461
00:36:41,121 --> 00:36:44,040
and interactions we have with our community at times.

462
00:36:44,800 --> 00:36:48,720
But then again, I do feel that we do have a job to do and a service to provide.

463
00:36:49,560 --> 00:36:56,320
And once emotions and feelings become involved with patient care,

464
00:36:56,321 --> 00:37:01,160
to an extent it can interfere with the proper and effective delivery

465
00:37:01,161 --> 00:37:04,160
of what is truly best for that patient in that situation.

466
00:37:05,320 --> 00:37:10,020
For those injuries and illnesses that do not require a call to 911

467
00:37:10,021 --> 00:37:12,020
or a trip to the emergency room,

468
00:37:12,640 --> 00:37:16,120
patients now have the option of going to urgent care centers.

469
00:37:17,760 --> 00:37:21,780
These centers have cropped up around the country over the last 20 years.

470
00:37:21,781 --> 00:37:28,780
They are walk-in clinics that can handle almost any type of medical problem, except the worst trauma cases.

471
00:37:28,781 --> 00:37:32,780
But they haven't lightened the load in the emergency rooms.

472
00:37:32,781 --> 00:37:40,780
There are urgent care centers that were designed to try to be lower in cost and provide, quote, faster care, unquote.

473
00:37:40,781 --> 00:37:44,780
But I think most people are still more comfortable coming to an emergency department.

474
00:37:44,781 --> 00:37:51,780
And it's hard to define what is a true emergency and what is urgent, but not emergent.

475
00:37:51,781 --> 00:37:55,920
And so when people come, they invariably think they're having one of the worst problems of their life,

476
00:37:55,960 --> 00:37:59,820
even though it may be just a severe sore throat or maybe a little dry cough.

477
00:38:00,120 --> 00:38:02,480
For you, that may be the emergency of a lifetime.

478
00:38:02,980 --> 00:38:08,540
And I think that urgent care centers have been convenience centers.

479
00:38:09,020 --> 00:38:17,020
But just as 7-Elevens have not reduced the business of the major grocery store chains,

480
00:38:17,021 --> 00:38:22,240
I don't think urgent care centers have affected the workload of the emergency department.

481
00:38:23,980 --> 00:38:27,260
We now return to modern marvels.

482
00:38:31,160 --> 00:38:38,200
The future of emergency medicine may be found on the highest mountain on Earth, Mount Everest.

483
00:38:39,540 --> 00:38:43,300
The Extreme Everest Expedition is in its second year.

484
00:38:43,301 --> 00:38:50,300
Its purpose is to uncover secrets to the human body's functioning in reaction to extreme and adverse conditions.

485
00:38:54,300 --> 00:39:01,300
Researchers from NASA and Yale University are conducting experiments with new medical devices and telemedicine.

486
00:39:01,301 --> 00:39:09,300
The Everest doctors gather the data from the climbers on the mountain and then transmit it back to Yale for instant evaluation.

487
00:39:09,301 --> 00:39:16,300
The impact of these experiments will soon be felt in emergency departments around the world.

488
00:39:16,301 --> 00:39:22,300
We'll be monitoring some of their basic vital signs, heart rate, temperature, movement,

489
00:39:22,301 --> 00:39:27,300
in addition to following their exact location through a GPS monitor.

490
00:39:27,301 --> 00:39:34,300
The doctors on Everest have set up a makeshift emergency room in one of the tents at base camp.

491
00:39:34,301 --> 00:39:40,140
They have most of the equipment necessary to handle any medical emergency short of a major trauma.

492
00:39:40,820 --> 00:39:46,240
They will certainly be able to provide a good level of care in a hands-on sense,

493
00:39:46,840 --> 00:39:54,200
but we are on a daily and sometimes twice daily basis going to be in communication with that medical team

494
00:39:54,201 --> 00:40:03,520
and review the clinical cases and discuss and decide what other treatment plans could be instituted.

495
00:40:04,300 --> 00:40:07,000
The base camp physicians perform

496
00:40:07,001 --> 00:40:09,780
phendoscopic examinations on the climbers

497
00:40:09,781 --> 00:40:11,340
to look behind the retina.

498
00:40:12,300 --> 00:40:14,220
Much can be learned about the effects

499
00:40:14,221 --> 00:40:15,540
of living at high altitude

500
00:40:15,541 --> 00:40:18,280
by checking the blood flow behind the eye.

501
00:40:18,880 --> 00:40:21,120
There are a lot of vision problems

502
00:40:21,121 --> 00:40:22,560
up there at altitude,

503
00:40:23,140 --> 00:40:25,700
and surprisingly, as I said,

504
00:40:25,760 --> 00:40:27,760
there's fairly little data or information

505
00:40:27,761 --> 00:40:29,040
on what to do about it.

506
00:40:29,041 --> 00:40:32,040
The Everest team uses digital microscopes

507
00:40:32,041 --> 00:40:37,580
to analyze the red and white blood cells and the changes that occur when the body is exposed

508
00:40:37,581 --> 00:40:43,420
to the rigors of being in the low oxygen environment.

509
00:40:43,421 --> 00:40:48,400
The Everest team uses a digital spirometer to measure how much air is in the lungs and

510
00:40:48,401 --> 00:40:53,140
how difficult it is for the climbers to breathe.

511
00:40:53,141 --> 00:40:58,220
And they use an iStat, which is used in trauma facilities to get a fast reading on the blood's

512
00:40:58,221 --> 00:40:59,800
properties.

513
00:40:59,801 --> 00:41:03,250
the blood sample has been placed in the i-stat.

514
00:41:03,251 --> 00:41:06,920
It gives the pH, percentages of oxygen saturation,

515
00:41:06,921 --> 00:41:13,000
potassium and ionized calcium, and the hematic rate to the doctors in less than 90 seconds.

516
00:41:13,001 --> 00:41:16,840
A complete blood count used to take more than 30 minutes in a hospital.

517
00:41:18,680 --> 00:41:22,526
The instruments and monitoring devices are small and lightweight.

518
00:41:22,527 --> 00:41:24,360
Every morning before they begin

519
00:41:24,361 --> 00:41:29,080
In their trek, the Everest climbers are hooked up to the leads that stick onto their chest,

520
00:41:29,520 --> 00:41:31,440
much like the electrocardiograph.

521
00:41:33,960 --> 00:41:39,160
The monitors are carried in a small biopack that the climbers wear around their waist.

522
00:41:39,860 --> 00:41:45,720
The climbers also swallow a large pill that records their internal temperature for up to 30 hours.

523
00:41:46,300 --> 00:41:49,880
The pill is a capsule that contains a tiny radio transmitter.

524
00:41:49,881 --> 00:41:51,460
Ah, lunch!

525
00:41:51,461 --> 00:41:58,660
The electrical components in the pill and the biopack send the data to a computer at base camp.

526
00:41:59,380 --> 00:42:05,520
And that information is then transmitted via a satellite system to Yale in real time.

527
00:42:06,340 --> 00:42:10,060
The doctors in New Haven, Connecticut schedule their rounds accordingly.

528
00:42:10,061 --> 00:42:22,680
The folks at Mount Everest will have two B phones, they're called, which provide 64 kilobit per second of data transmission capability.

529
00:42:23,080 --> 00:42:31,720
That is enough to provide with reasonable video and audio as well as transmitting some of these larger data files.

530
00:42:31,721 --> 00:42:35,880
Once that gets up into the satellite system, then it comes back down,

531
00:42:35,940 --> 00:42:39,720
and we'll be transmitting mostly through the ISDN types of technologies,

532
00:42:39,860 --> 00:42:45,100
as well as using the Internet and doing some testing with the Internet 2 system as well.

533
00:42:45,600 --> 00:42:52,360
The results of this research will one day play a definitive role on the front lines of emergency medicine.

534
00:42:53,040 --> 00:42:56,100
This is related to emergency medicine in the sense that

535
00:42:56,101 --> 00:43:02,100
Emergency medicine is a very, very acute way that we practice medicine.

536
00:43:02,101 --> 00:43:12,100
And the only thing different between emergency medicine and day-to-day medicine is the order of magnitude of the injury or disease and time.

537
00:43:12,101 --> 00:43:14,100
The tempo is much faster.

538
00:43:14,101 --> 00:43:20,100
Therefore, technologies that we have been developing in the past that we use in doctors' offices,

539
00:43:20,101 --> 00:43:26,580
as we make them smaller and we make them instantly available to acquire the information.

540
00:43:27,300 --> 00:43:29,940
Therefore, they can migrate immediately to the emergency room.

541
00:43:33,080 --> 00:43:37,000
What we're really trying to look forward to in the future is,

542
00:43:37,260 --> 00:43:41,800
how do you detect what's going on with a person's body?

543
00:43:42,380 --> 00:43:45,960
How do you decide what is the real true problem there?

544
00:43:45,961 --> 00:43:54,260
And then as important as anything, how do you do something about it without that person necessarily having to come into some type of health care facility?

545
00:43:55,140 --> 00:44:03,400
For those who will visit an emergency room, it will most likely be a scary, if not traumatic, experience.

546
00:44:03,800 --> 00:44:15,180
But with the expertise of hospital staff and the latest in diagnostic devices and treatments, that visit could be the best trip of their life.

547
00:44:15,960 --> 00:44:21,420
I feel comfortable saying this because I really believe it.

548
00:44:22,160 --> 00:44:27,660
The trauma systems, the systems we have for injury prevention that we're just developing now,

549
00:44:27,880 --> 00:44:30,520
when you actually come into an emergency department,

550
00:44:31,180 --> 00:44:35,080
you can feel comfortable and very confident that there are protocols

551
00:44:35,081 --> 00:44:39,440
and there are practices that have been put in place that you will be taken care of.

552
00:44:40,400 --> 00:44:44,720
But despite advances in technology and trauma care procedures,

553
00:44:44,721 --> 00:44:50,820
the fate of an emergency room patient still rests in the hands of a doctor.

554
00:44:54,480 --> 00:44:55,520
The Wild.

